it('reports summed usage across every provider call and nothing when none report', async () => {
  const registry = new ToolRegistry();
  registry.register(
    {name: 'lookup', description: 'Looks something up.', inputSchema: {type: 'object'}},
    async () => 'found'
  );

  let receivedUsage: UsageMetadata | undefined;
  const orchestrator = createChatOrchestrator(
    createScriptedProvider([
      toolRound(usageFor(11, 2), 'first-call'),
      toolRound(undefined, 'second-call'),
      [
        {type: 'text', text: 'The answer.'},
        {type: 'stop', stopReason: 'end_turn', usage: usageFor(7, 5)},
      ],
    ]),
    new PromptBuilder({identity: 'Acceptance test assistant.'}),
    registry,
    {hooks: {afterModelResponse: async (_session, _text, usage) => void (receivedUsage = usage)}}
  );

  await orchestrator.processMessage({...session, entries: [...session.entries]});

  expect(receivedUsage).toEqual(usageFor(18, 7));

  const noUsageOrchestrator = createChatOrchestrator(
    createScriptedProvider([[{type: 'stop', stopReason: 'end_turn'}]]),
    new PromptBuilder({identity: 'Acceptance test assistant.'}),
    undefined,
    {hooks: {afterModelResponse: async (_session, _text, usage) => void (receivedUsage = usage)}}
  );

  await noUsageOrchestrator.processMessage({...session, entries: [...session.entries]});

  expect(receivedUsage).toBeUndefined();
});
# Definition of Done

> what the hook receives is the usage of the final provider call alone.

**Strategy: `direct`** — `createChatOrchestrator` exposes the real provider, tool registry, and `afterModelResponse` hook seams, so a scripted provider can observe the complete turn without mocks or source-text assertions.

## Done when these pass

- [ ] **reports summed usage across every provider call and nothing when none report** — the hook receives the sum of the first and final provider usages, while a middle round with no usage neither produces `undefined`/`NaN` nor contributes tokens, and an all-unreported turn remains `undefined`.
      `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts`
- [ ] **receives collected response text and usage** — the existing no-tool test remains unedited and pins that a direct turn reports exactly its provider usage.
      `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts`

## Facets

- [ ] Make the public hook type communicate that its usage argument is the per-turn sum, without introducing `any`.
- [ ] Aggregate reported usage across all provider calls, preserving `undefined` when no call reports usage and preserving the no-tool result.
- [ ] Replace the spec's under-counting description and add an Unreleased changelog entry describing the larger usage existing consumers will record.

## Out of scope

- Per-round usage breakdowns, audit or billing consumers, non-provider token accounting, and the `stop` chunk's existing usage shape.
- A `beforeSession` that throws reaches the caller as a rejection, and also runs `onError` with the thrown error and then `afterSession`, because the call sits inside the same `try`/`finally` as the rest of the turn. An `afterSession` hook called under this condition can infer the session never advanced past `beforeSession`: no user-input hooks ran, no model response was generated, and no tool rounds were started — a cleanup hook that allocates in `beforeSession` and frees in `afterSession` will therefore always see the allocation and the release on the same error path ([validated by: runs onError then afterSession when it throws, and still rejects to the caller](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts#L116)).

## Engine startup

- The engine's `start()` method rejects the returned promise when it cannot bind the configured port, rather than emitting an unhandled error event that would take the process down ([validated by: rejects instead of taking the process down with an unhandled error event](../../src/config.test.ts#L174)).

## Measured semantics

These properties of `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` are recorded so the hook features specify against real behaviour.

- The `catch` gates `onError` on `error instanceof Error` ([chatOrchestrator.ts#L150](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts#L150)), so a rejection that is not an `Error` — a thrown string or object — bypasses `onError` entirely while still propagating to the caller.
- `afterModelResponse` is called once per user message after the round loop exits, and receives usage summed across every provider call in the turn; a round that reports no usage contributes nothing, and a turn where no round reports usage receives no usage ([validated by: reports summed usage across every provider call and nothing when none report](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L38)).
